  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Opioid use disorder on buprenorphine, after a brief return to use (F11.20)</content>. A brief return to use caused by a gap in medication, not by a failure of treatment, managed safely with test strips, a companion, and naloxone. A return to use is an expected part of recovery for many people and is a reason to strengthen treatment, not to stop it. Urine test today shows buprenorphine and norfentanyl, which fits use six days ago. She keeps her place in treatment. Dr. Ortiz reviewed the plan by phone today and agrees. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide or self-harm; overdose risk discussed openly. [S]<list><item>Continue buprenorphine with naloxone 16 mg daily.</item><item>Prior authorization renewed for twelve months; pharmacy chain that can fill in any state; prescription filled a week before each tour; a travel letter and the clinic number for any problem.</item><item>Extended-release monthly buprenorphine injection offered as a way to avoid pharmacy gaps; she would rather stay on films for now and will think about it.</item><item>Naloxone replaced; new test strips.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Alcohol use disorder on acamprosate (F10.20)</content>. PEth up from 96 to 164 ng/mL with heavier drinking on tour; AST 44, ALT 33, GGT 61 U/L. [S]<list><item>Continue acamprosate and thiamine.</item><item>Plan with her counselor for nights on tour: non-alcoholic drinks after shows and a call to her peer coach.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Low mood after the return to use</content>. PHQ-9 11. On interview, the low mood, poor sleep, and shame began with the return to use and the tour; she does not describe a depressive episode, and there is no history suggesting mania. No thoughts of suicide. [S]<list><item>Weekly in-person counseling now that she is home.</item><item>PHQ-9 again at the preventive visit in six weeks.</item><item>The safety plan reviewed and updated with her.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Buprenorphine dose, adherence, cravings, withdrawal, and side effects</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Gap of three days from a lapsed prior authorization, then restarted at 16 mg; no precipitated withdrawal</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Visit frequency</td><td>Every 1 to 2 weeks in the first month after starting, then every 2 to 4 weeks until stable</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Video check-ins every two weeks kept since the five-week visit; seen in person within days of her call</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Urine drug testing, including fentanyl, explained as part of care rather than as a test of trust</td><td>At the start, then about monthly early in treatment and as needed</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Buprenorphine, norbuprenorphine, and norfentanyl, consistent with what she reported</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Prescription drug monitoring program check</td><td>Before each buprenorphine prescription</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No other prescriptions</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Naloxone at home and in her bag, and a trained companion</td><td>At the start; check and replace at each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Not used; replaced with new test strips</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Overdose prevention: never using alone and fentanyl test strips</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>She tested the supply and used with a companion and naloxone; reinforced without judgment</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Prescription continuity on tour (pharmacy, prior authorization, travel letter)</td><td>Before each tour</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Prior authorization renewed for twelve months; national pharmacy chain; early fill and travel letter</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Counseling and peer recovery support</td><td>Offered to everyone; not a condition of medication</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Weekly in-person counseling from now; peer coach</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Drinking and PEth</td><td>PEth at intake and at each check while drinking continues; AUDIT-C yearly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PEth 164 ng/mL, up from 96, with heavier drinking on tour</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Acamprosate adherence and kidney function</td><td>Kidney function before starting; adherence every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Taking it; midday reminder working</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Thiamine</td><td>While drinking heavily</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Taking thiamine daily</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Liver tests (AST, ALT, GGT, albumin, bilirubin)</td><td>At intake, then every 1 to 3 months while drinking is heavy, and yearly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>AST 44, ALT 33, GGT 61 U/L</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>FIB-4 fibrosis score</td><td>At intake; repeat in 6 to 12 months with ongoing drinking, and every 1 to 2 years</td><td>Up to date</td><td>1.18 at intake; repeat at six months</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screen (PHQ-9)</td><td>At intake and at each recovery visit while symptoms are present</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-9 11, item 9 scored 0; interview does not show a depressive episode</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Mood Disorder Questionnaire</td><td>At the evaluation of a new depressive episode</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No depressive episode on interview and no history suggesting mania</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide and overdose risk review, safety plan, and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No thoughts of suicide or self-harm; overdose risk after the gap reviewed; the safety plan updated; the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline in her phone</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Stay on buprenorphine without interruption through six months, including through any return to use. In progress; target 2026-10-19 [S]</item><item>Naloxone at home and in her bag, and a friend trained to use it, by the one-week visit. Outcome by 2026-04-27: Two naloxone two-packs dispensed; her friend trained [S]</item><item>Reduce drinking to PEth below 20 ng/mL, or to the level she chooses, by six months. In progress; target 2026-10-19 [S]</item><item>Hepatitis A and B vaccine series completed by six months. In progress; target 2026-10-19 [S]</item><item>PHQ-9 score below 5 by six months. In progress; target 2026-10-19 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: The ASAM National Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Opioid Use Disorder: 2020 Focused Update (2020); Treatment Options for Substance Use Disorder (medications for opioid and alcohol use disorders) (2026); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Substance Use Disorders (2021); Diagnosis and Treatment of Alcohol-Associated Liver Diseases: 2019 Practice Guidance From the American Association for the Study of Liver Diseases (2019); Depression and Suicide Risk in Adults: Screening (2023); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Weekly counseling. Preventive visit in six weeks with the PHQ-9. Call the clinic the same day if a refill fails or cravings rise. Next scheduled visit 2026-08-31: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

